WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:07.043
So far the bulb's output was a set of active glomeruli.

2
00:00:07.943 --> 00:00:09.323
Is that all the cortex receives?

3
00:00:10.417 --> 00:00:15.717
No. The bulb reshapes the pattern with inhibition, and the pattern carries timing.

4
00:00:16.817 --> 00:00:22.317
The key contact is a reciprocal dendrodendritic synapse, one dendrite signalling to

5
00:00:22.317 --> 00:00:23.317
another and back.

6
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:28.290
A mitral or tufted dendrite releases glutamate onto the interneuron.

7
00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:32.390
The interneuron releases GABA back onto the same dendrite.

8
00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:36.010
The loop can inhibit the cell that excited it.

9
00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:37.770
That is recurrent inhibition.

10
00:00:38.883 --> 00:00:43.423
One interneuron touches many output cells, so it can also inhibit neighbours.

11
00:00:44.343 --> 00:00:45.583
That is lateral inhibition.

12
00:00:46.717 --> 00:00:49.837
Granule cells are not the only source of this inhibition.

13
00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:54.547
This resembles the lateral inhibition of sensory coding.

14
00:00:55.287 --> 00:00:56.647
But the bulb is not a retina.

15
00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:02.640
Each mitral cell is influenced by a few scattered glomeruli, not a ring of

16
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:03.640
neighbours around it.

17
00:01:04.483 --> 00:01:08.903
Now timing. A sniff is a cycle of breathing in and breathing out.

18
00:01:09.867 --> 00:01:12.287
A spike's phase is where in that cycle it falls.

19
00:01:13.317 --> 00:01:18.097
A review of bulb coding concluded that the relative timing of activity in the bulb

20
00:01:18.097 --> 00:01:19.777
conveys odour information.

21
00:01:20.967 --> 00:01:25.007
Whether and how downstream circuits use that timing has been debated.

22
00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:30.010
Tufted and mitral cells lock to opposite phases of the sniff.

23
00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:32.670
They are parallel channels, not copies.

24
00:01:33.733 --> 00:01:35.693
Sister cells make a sharp test.

25
00:01:36.553 --> 00:01:39.333
Do cells that share one glomerulus send the same message?

26
00:01:40.383 --> 00:01:43.943
Dhawale and colleagues recorded in anaesthetized adult mice.

27
00:01:44.983 --> 00:01:50.123
A light-based method found which recorded cells received input from one parent

28
00:01:50.123 --> 00:01:51.123
glomerulus.

29
00:01:51.817 --> 00:01:56.397
So sisters were defined by their input, not by how they responded to odours.

30
00:01:57.367 --> 00:01:58.627
Pause and predict.

31
00:01:59.847 --> 00:02:00.847
Sisters share one input.

32
00:02:01.807 --> 00:02:02.807
Will their rates match?

33
00:02:03.447 --> 00:02:05.007
Will their timing against the breath match?

34
00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:14.170
Counting mean spikes, sister responses were correlated.

35
00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:19.550
But against the breathing cycle, an odour shifted the timing of sisters differently.

36
00:02:20.683 --> 00:02:22.083
Rate made them look alike.

37
00:02:22.763 --> 00:02:28.003
Timing did not. A shared glomerulus is a shared address, not a shared message.

38
00:02:28.933 --> 00:02:30.473
Read this within its limits.

39
00:02:31.413 --> 00:02:34.853
The mice were anaesthetized, and mitral and tufted cells were pooled.

40
00:02:35.917 --> 00:02:39.257
So some timing differences may reflect cell type or state.

41
00:02:40.267 --> 00:02:43.527
So the cortex receives a timed map shaped by inhibition.

42
00:02:44.427 --> 00:02:48.887
It turns that into a distributed code that its network reshapes and that drifts.
